 privacy and reliability. Additionally, CBS collects data through its own surveys. New data sources such as big data are also increasingly forming the basis for statistics.", "title": "How does CBS collect data? - The Netherlands in numbers 2022", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/rapportages/2023/cbs-multi-annual-programme-2024-2028/strategic-objective-3-maintaining-high-quality", "snippet": "Although CBS seeks to use government records and innovative data collection methods as much as possible, requesting data by means of surveys remains indispensable. CBS will continue to take explicit account of the burden caused by data requests and aims to limit the increase in the administrative burden as much as possible. CBS puts enterprises at the centre of its data collection. Good services, predictable requests, easy submission and reuse of business data are essential aspects.", "title": "Strategic objective 3: Maintaining high quality - CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/participants-survey/businesses/overview/monthly-survey", "snippet": "As required by the Statistics Netherlands'' law, Statistics Netherland uses as much data as possible from other institutions'' files. We examine the usability, timeliness and completeness of the data. If the files do not contain the required data or do not contain it in time, we may request the data from businesses. Under the Law, you are obliged to deliver the requested data on time, completely and free of charge.", "title": "Monthly survey - CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/participants-survey/businesses/more-about-cbs-surveys/enforcement", "snippet": "CBS provides insight into social and economic issues using statistical information. To obtain this information, we collect as much of the requisite data as possible from other bodies, both government and otherwise, such as the Tax and Customs Administration and the Chamber of Commerce. If these bodies do not have the requisite data, CBS is authorised to legally oblige companies to supply their data. This obligation has been laid down in the Statistics Netherlands Act.", "title": "Enforcement - CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/item?sc_itemid=aa98a4e5-734a-495a-ad11-8cbcacb11573&sc_lang=en-gb", "snippet": "As required by the Statistics Netherlands'' law, Statistics Netherlands uses as much data as possible from other institutions'' files. We examine the usability, timeliness and completeness of the data. If the files do not contain the required data or do not contain it in time, we may request the data from businesses. Under the Law, you are obliged to deliver the requested data on time, completely and free of charge.", "title": "International Trade in Services | CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/about-us/who-we-are/our-organisation/operational-management/government-related-information-management", "snippet": "A WOO request has no form requirements, but must meet the following three conditions:1. the request must be as specific as possible about the type of information that is requested; 2. the request must be related to information contained in actual documents; 3. the request must be related to information that is available at Statistics Netherlands (CBS).", "title": "Government-related information management - CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/participants-survey/businesses/overview/annual-statistics", "snippet": "For this survey, we ask both large and small businesses and self-employed people to complete a questionnaire. The results of this survey provide insight into economic growth and are used by business, government and science.", "title": "Annual Statistics | CBS", "provider": "diy"}, {"url": "https://www.cbs.nl/nl-nl/over-ons/onderzoek-en-innovatie/nieuwsberichten/open-data/valkuilen-en-vangnetten-bij-het-publiceren-van-open-data", "snippet": "CBS loopt in de ontwikkeling van methodologie en software op dit gebied internationaal voorop. De twee docenten, Eric Schulte Nordholt en Peter-Paul de Wolf, deelden tijdens de cursus hun kennis op het gebied van open data met verschillende nationale overheidspartijen.", "title": "Valkuilen en vangnetten bij het publiceren van open data - CBS", "provider": "diy"}]');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO phase2_researcher_runs (id,run_id,pair_run_id,question_id,country_code,retry_count,answer,answer_explanation,evidence_quote,source_url,retrieval_confidence,answer_confidence,search_queries_used,fetched_urls,domain_trust_score,language_route_used,notes,failure_mode,input_tokens,output_tokens,wall_clock_ms,estimated_cost_usd,condition_label,prompt_version_id,model_version,raw_response,created_at,search_provider_calls,experiment_id,search_snippets) VALUES (8553,'d33b591d-8bc1-41bb-b348-3970d734682e','07d61a85-9b76-49f3-b509-55afb17ca141','I22','NL',2,'no','The search results show the Netherlands publishes environmental statistics and supports open data accessibility, but provide no evidence of documented research studies, impact assessments, or systematic investigations quantifying the environmental impact created by open data reuse.','The statistics produced by CBS are a public good; public access is therefore a fundamental element of its statistics. As also prescribed by the European Statistics Code of Practice, CBS actively publishes all results of its statistical research on open data.','https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/diversen/2022/cbs-multi-annual-programme-2024-2028',0.95,0.6,'["open data circulaire economie milieueffecten evaluatie Nederland", "European Green Deal open data reuse environmental outcomes studies", "site:overheid.nl open data impact assessment duurzaamheid resultaten"]','["https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/rapportages/2024/measuring-the-green-transition-with-business-tendency-surveys/1-introduction", "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/rapportages/2024/measuring-the-green-transition-with-business-tendency-surveys?onepage=true", "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/diversen/2022/cbs-multi-annual-programme-2024-2028?onepage=true", "https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/longread/discussion-papers/2023/developing-a-material-flow-monitor-for-the-netherlands-from-national-statistical-data?onepage=true"]',1.0,'native','source_url not among search snippets',NULL,6223,1008,13007,0.011263,'haiku_h45',22,'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001','I need to search more directly for evidence of whether the Netherlands has documented data on open data''s environmental impact. The previous search focused on general Green Deal and environmental statistics, not specifically on open data reuse impact. Let me analyze what we have and what''s missing.

The current snippets show:
- CBS publishes environmental/sustainability statistics and open data
- The Netherlands has programs measuring green transition
- But nothing explicitly states whether there are studies quantifying the *impact of open data reuse* on environmental challenges

The question asks specifically: "Is any data on the impact created by open data on environmental challenges...available in your country such as from research studies, impact assessments, or other systematic investigations that quantify the results or broader effects of open data being reused?"

The key distinction is: Do they have studies showing that *reusing open data* created environmental benefits? Not just that they publish environmental data.

Looking at the snippets:
- [3] mentions CBS improving "accessibility and hence use of our data" and "open data" but doesn''t claim impact assessments on environmental outcomes from reuse
- [4] mentions monitoring for circular economy using "European statistics" but doesn''t attribute environmental impact to open data reuse
- [1] and [2] describe EU Green Deal objectives but don''t mention open data impact studies

Based on the available evidence, I cannot find a verbatim quote that demonstrates the existence of research studies, impact assessments, or systematic investigations specifically quantifying the results or broader effects of open data being reused for environmental purposes in the Netherlands.

The evidence does not support answering "yes" with a credible source. The appropriate answer given the instruction to commit to a label is **"no"** — there is no evidence in these results of such impact studies being publicly available.

