ng Orders stipulating that they must be answered within three working days."]','Evidence fit failure: The quoted passage describes open data mechanisms and initiatives in place, but does not establish that these mechanisms are actually having a measurable impact on transparency and accountability. Counter-evidence from independent searches reveals significant documented failures in transparency and accountability in Malta''s public administration (denied FOI requests, unanswered parliamentary questions, limited OGP plan completion, minimal government-civil society dialogue), which directly contradicts the affirmative answer. The Researcher''s claim conflates the existence of open data infrastructure with demonstrated impact on transparency.','Public bodies regularly deny FOI requests from media on arbitrary grounds. [...] Some 750 parliamentary questions submitted to government ministers since March 2022 had gone unanswered despite Parliament''s Standing Orders stipulating that they must be answered.','https://www.article19.org/resources/malta-media-battle-for-access-to-public-information/','Malta open data reuse cases transparency impact examples government accountability',NULL,6013,668,9498,0.009353,'haiku_h45',23,'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001','```json
