ublished datasets cover the complete time series from first publication to today, which is what the question asks. The Researcher''s own notes concede this is a single dataset rather than an explicit national policy.','Een klein aantal reeksen heeft als startpunt 1900 en beslaat de volledige periode tot en met heden','https://data.overheid.nl/dataset/173-tijdreeksen-sociale-zekerheid','data.overheid.nl OR CBS beleid volledige tijdreeksen behouden completeness policy open data',NULL,4038,857,17457,0.041615,'opus_o48',23,'claude-opus-4-8','The evidence is about a single dataset (social security time series) reaching back to 1900, not a national policy or systematic effort to ensure complete time series across published data. The Researcher even acknowledges this in their notes. The question asks whether the *country* undertakes efforts to ensure complete coverage — a single dataset''s start date does not confirm an organisational measure.

Counter-evidence snippet [1] actually shows CBS discusses series continuity, but nothing establishes a policy of maintaining complete series to the present. The evidence fit is insufficient for the specific question.

